        "evidence/dm-default-key-local-review.json",
        "evidence/dm-default-key-check.json",
        "SECURITY.md: Original block crypto and metadata encryption"
      ],
      "patch_sha256": "63a37af8a8b030dd9c46dea86f4195d2f285eb4b0a7918353feaf48af386312f"
    },
    {
      "commit": "cb39ec0c1040e89d14ae0852454f8fd64a704118",
      "disposition": "adapted-net-state",
      "rationale": "Complete ten-file original metadata-encryption target patch examined, including all skip-flag producers, cloning and merge checks. Retain evolved dm-default-key: local goldfish fstab requests metadata encryption, vold creates DmTargetDefaultKey and libdm emits its supported modern syntax. Retain key/status redaction and wiping, current key-type/size and block-device APIs, ioctl size restrictions, target-relative IV geometry and skip flags for both fscrypt and raw encrypted F2FS GC. Existing 2d4754f38b2b rejects unaligned/overflowed IV ranges and ensures a nonzero DUN width; c45528667984 preserves fallback clone skip state. Keep native zone serialization rather than the unrelated unavailable ORDERED_HWQ flag. The design assumes file-encryption keys are at least as protected as metadata keys; skip flags are trusted kernel state, not a userspace request to bypass encryption. Native Linux had no dm-default-key target to replace. Actual SDK encrypted I/O and wrapped hardware remain unverified.",
      "result_commits": [
        "2fbc2da4ade330c86d97491696f9223c9df7bc8f",
        "2d4754f38b2b5e7051e1324aca6cf44fec374cbd",
